Security Principles
Authorization Code is exchanged only via backend — avoids leaking tokens in browser.
Access Token is stored securely on backend or frontend (with proper PKCE).
Refresh Token (if issued) stays only on backend or secure cookie.



 Common Authentication Types
Type	Description
1. Basic Authentication	Username + password sent as Base64 in HTTP headers
2. Token-Based Authentication	Client logs in, gets a token (e.g. JWT), uses it in future requests
3. OAuth2 / OpenID Connect	Delegated login via Authorization Server (e.g. Google, Keycloak)
4. API Key	A secret key passed in headers or query params
5. Session-Based Auth	Server creates a session ID stored in browser cookies
6. Multi-Factor Auth (MFA)	Combines password + OTP or biometric
7. Certificate-Based Auth	Uses client SSL/TLS certificates
8. SAML (for enterprise SSO)	XML-based authentication/SSO protocol
9. Biometric Auth	Face ID, fingerprint, etc.
10. Social Login	Login with Google, Facebook, etc. (OAuth2-based)

Common Authentication Types Between Microservices
Auth Type	Description
1. JWT (OAuth2 Access Tokens)	Most popular. Each request carries a signed JWT (Bearer Token).
2. mTLS (Mutual TLS)	Both client and server present certificates; very secure.
3. API Keys	Simple, but less secure; used for internal services.
4. Basic Auth	Username/password (base64) — mostly deprecated.
5. Service Mesh Auth (e.g., Istio + Envoy)	Handles identity and encryption transparently via sidecars.
6. SPIFFE/SPIRE	Identity framework for workload authentication in microservices.
7. OPA (Open Policy Agent)	Often used for authZ, but can verify tokens too.


