This is exactly how a frontend app prepares for OAuth2 Authorization Code flow with PKCE.
code_verifier stays private
code_challenge is sent to the auth server
During token exchange, the app sends back the code_verifier
Auth server hashes it and compares with original code_challenge
This ensures that only the app that started the flow can finish it. 🔒














Authentication between microserives
✅ Most Common Setup (Real-world microservice apps):
🔐 JWT-Based Auth (Token Propagation)
User logs in to gateway (via OAuth2 / Identity Provider).
Gateway generates a JWT access token.
JWT is forwarded to downstream services.
Each service:
  Validates the token (using public key or JWKS)
  Extracts user/service identity
  Enforces scopes or roles
Pros: Stateless, decoupled, scalable
Cons: Token validation logic needed in each service (or via shared lib/filter)

🔒 mTLS for Service Identity
Each service has a certificate signed by a trusted CA
TLS is established both ways → authenticates both sides
Used in service meshes like Istio, Linkerd, etc.
Pros: Very strong identity/auth
Cons: Operationally complex (cert rotation, setup)

✅ Hybrid (Best Practice Today)
Layer	Auth Method
Gateway → Service	JWT (with scopes/claims)
Service → Service	JWT (propagation) or mTLS (for identity)
Zero-trust style	Add mTLS + per-call auth checks (OPA, scopes)
